 launched from the US West Coast. InSight (Interior Exploration using Seismic Investigations) carried twin CubeSats MarCO-A + MarCO-B for real-time EDL telemetry relay — the first cubesats to leave Earth orbit.",{"met_days":64,"type":65,"label":66,"description":67},0.005,"tli_or_tmi","Trans-Mars injection","Centaur upper stage burn places InSight + MarCO on a 6.5-month Mars transfer.",{"met_days":69,"type":70,"label":71,"description":72},22,"tcm","TCM-1","First post-TMI dispersion cleanup.",{"met_days":74,"type":70,"label":75,"description":76},47.15,"TCM-2","Mid-cruise refinement.",{"met_days":78,"type":70,"label":79,"description":76},80,"TCM-3",{"met_days":81,"type":70,"label":82,"description":83},200,"TCM-4 — final pre-EDL burn","Tightens approach targeting for Elysium Planitia EDL site.",{"met_days":12,"type":85,"label":86,"description":87},"arrival","Mars approach + cruise-stage separation","2018-11-26. The MarCO cubesats arrived independently and relayed live EDL telemetry back to Earth — proof-of-concept for cubesat deep-space mission support.",{"met_days":89,"type":90,"label":91,"description":92},205.005,"edl_or_oi","Mars lander touchdown — Elysium Planitia","Heritage Phoenix EDL architecture (parachute + retrorockets, no sky crane) — InSight is a stationary lander, not a rover, so it could use the simpler legacy approach. Landed 2018-11-26 at 4.5°N, 135.6°E. Deployed SEIS seismometer (made the first Marsquake observations) and HP³ heat-flow probe (which jammed and never reached target depth). Final transmission 2022-12-15; NASA declared the mission over 2022-12-21 after two missed comm attempts confirmed the solar arrays could no longer sustain power.",{"source_tier":94,"reference_frame":95,"departure_body":96,"arrival_body":97,"transfer_type":98,"tcm_count":44,"waypoints_helio_au":99},"tier_1_5_hybrid","heliocentric_ecliptic_J2000","earth","mars","type1_lambert",[100,103,107,111,115,119,123,127,131,135,139,142,146,150,154,158,162,166,170,174,178,182,186,189