3,434,59,435],182.6408,1.475955,0.120135,[437,438,59,439],184.8151,1.478071,0.148335,[441,442,59,443],186.9894,1.479599,0.176475,[445,446,59,447],189.1637,1.480507,0.204542,[449,450,59,451],191.338,1.480756,0.232517,[453,454,59,455],193.5123,1.480432,0.260402,[457,458,59,459],195.6866,1.479479,0.288177,[461,462,59,463],197.8609,1.477907,0.315831,[81,465,59,466],1.475815,0.342922,[468,469,59,470],202.2095,1.473013,0.370754,[89,472,59,473],1.468636,0.405766,[475,476,59,477],206.5581,1.466785,0.412411,[479,476,59,477],208.7324,[481,476,59,477],210.9067,[483,476,59,477],213.081,[485,476,59,477],215.2553,"InSight","LANDER · FLOWN","First to detect marsquakes; first interplanetary launch from US West Coast","InSight gave Mars its first stethoscope. Its seismometer, deployed by a robotic arm onto the regolith, recorded over 1,300 marsquakes — including a magnitude-5 in 2022, the largest ever detected on another planet. The data revealed Mars's interior structure: a 1,800-km-radius molten core, a thick crust, and surprising mantle layering. The mole heat-flow experiment failed when the regolith proved too cohesive for its self-hammering design. The mission ended when dust accumulation on solar panels reduced power below operational threshold.",[491,495,497,500,504,509],{"met":59,"label":492,"note":493,"type":494},"LAUNCH","Atlas V 401 from Vandenberg AFB — first interplanetary mission ever launched from the US West Coast.","nominal",{"met":69,"label":71,"note":496,"type":494},"First mid-course correction.",{"met":12,"label":498,"note":499,"type":494},"EDL","Atmospheric entry, parachute, terminal-descent rocket landing at Elysium Planitia.",{"met":501,"label":502,"note":503,"type":494},205.01,"TOUCHDOWN","Lander arrives 2018-11-26 19:54 UTC.",{"met":505,"label":506,"note":507,"type":508},228,"SEISMOMETER DEPLOYED","Robotic arm places SEIS instrument on the surface; its wind shield was added in February 2019.","info",{"met":510,"label":511,"note":512,"type":513},1683,"MISSION END","Final low-power transmission 2022-12-15. Solar panels too dust-covered to recharge.","warning","/orrery/images/missions/insight/01.webp"],"uses":{"params":["id"]}}]}
